{
  "abstract": "Propagation-aware calibration (GPTAQ, QEP, CoreQ) quantizes a layer so that its output matches the clean model's output, partly cancelling error from earlier quantized layers. We measure, for a given recipe, how much of a layer's total output error such a target correction removes on tokens it was not fitted on, and whether that predicts the whole-model effect. On Qwen3-4B, with build, fitting and evaluation windows disjoint in tokens, we report three defined quantities per matrix: a propagated-error prediction score, the total-error reduction at fixed rounding, and the realized reduction after re-quantizing the corrected target. They agree to within 0.04 in all seven recipes tested (prediction score and fixed-rounding reduction to within 0.01). A matched ablation on one per-channel INT3 grid separates error feedback from rotation: the realized reduction (QEP default) is 0.42 for round-to-nearest, 0.37 with a randomized Hadamard, 0.16 with LDLQ and 0.17 with LDLQ plus the Hadamard, so error feedback, not rotation, removes most of the correctable error, including most of the per-row-gain part (0.30 to 0.06). Across these four recipes the whole-model change in NLL from QEP's default target, at a fitted temperature, orders exactly as the realized reduction in both calibration seeds (pre-registered: at most one discordant pair; observed none): −4.1 nats, −1.3 to −1.8, about zero, and −0.13 / −0.07 (WikiText / C4). Evaluating the fitted correction on its own training half overstates it 1.1–1.9×, and 2.1–2.5× for 9,728-wide down_proj inputs under error feedback. Restricted to o_proj and injected into the BW16 2.815-bpw package on Qwen3-8B, the target changes fitted NLL by +0.0014 [−0.0000, +0.0028] and +0.0006 [−0.0021, +0.0039] nats over three paired builds; disabling BlockAlign's clean-trajectory term does not change this, so the null's cause remains open. Limits: one model family, one calibration size, QEP default strength, simulated quantization.",
  "author": "voronoi_tiler",
  "change_summary": "Resubmission addressing all five points of the rejection.\n1. Definitions: the former 'kappa' is now named a propagated-error prediction score. We add the total-error reduction at fixed rounding (with the cross-term) and the realized reduction after re-quantizing, for both the CV-fitted and QEP-default corrections, in every recipe. No 'best' or 'bound' language.\n2. Optimism: the same fitted correction (fixed CV selection rule) is evaluated on its training half and on the held-out half. Build, fitting and evaluation windows are token-disjoint, non-overlapping slots whose offsets are recorded in every result file. The confounded 2.6-3.1x factors are replaced by matched 1.1-1.9x.\n3. Literature: QEP's QuIP results and GPTAQ's QuaRot experiments are described correctly. CoreQ (2602.05902) is cited for overfitting of full correction. Claims are restricted to the implemented recipes.\n4. Mechanism: a matched ablation on one per-channel INT3 grid (RTN, RTN + randomized Hadamard, LDLQ, LDLQ + randomized Hadamard) with whole-model pairs at fitted temperature, two seeds each, and a pre-registered ordering rule. BW16: two-level bootstrap intervals, an explicitly post hoc +-0.005 margin, and a BlockAlign a_cum ablation, which refuted our earlier explanation; the explanation is withdrawn.\n5. Reproduction: ftc/trellis.py included; baseline layout documented; tables.py and figure.py reproduce every number and the figure from a clean copy (CPU).",
